  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1116 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nNorth to northeast winds have brought cooler air into southwest\nKansas today. This wedge of cool air will be very slow to erode \neven after the cool high pressure moves off to the east. A storm\nsystem will close off to our south by Saturday night, with\nabundant moisture in place for light rain. Any heavy rains\nshould stay well south given the stably stratified lower to mid\nlevels and lack of strong thermal advections. The various \nensemble means have settled on a solution that would bring minor\namounts of rain to I-70 and up to .5\" of rain along the \nOklahoma state line. In between in places like Lakin, Dodge City\nand Jetmore, expect amounts between .1\" and .4\", with exact \namounts depending on where the northern edge of the rain shield\nsets up. Per grand ensemble, chances for over 1\" of rain are \n10% or less along the Oklahoma state line and near 0% farther \nnorth, giving confidence that this will not be a heavy rain \nevent. That said, median November precipitation at Garden City \nand Dodge City is only .31\" and .42\" respectively, so even a \nthird of an inch is respectable for this time of year.\n\nAfter the aforementioned system passes, there is no strong upper\nlevel trough passage and associated front that would scour out\nthe cool and moist air. A south wind is not always a warm wind\nin the cool season since downslope flow tends to be negligible.\nTherefore, expect highs below below climatology in the 40s \nSunday even after the rain ends, with lower to mid 50s for \nMonday and Tuesday. Overnight lows will be rather mild and above\nclimatology since cloud cover, relatively high dewpoints and \nsouth winds will inhibit diurnal cooling. However, by Wednesday \na shortwave trough traversing the northern plains may be enough \nto push a dry downslope front across the high plains. How far \neast this warm plume gets ahead of the associated cold front is \nquestionable. But far western Kansas may reach into the 60s to \nnear 70 on Wednesday, with 50s to near 60 in central Kansas.\n\nThe various model suites still indicate a large upper level\ntrough developing across the western USA by late next week.\nSurface troughing and south winds should bring moisture back\ninto western Kansas by Thursday and Friday, possibly leading to\nanother period of cloudy weather. The increasing moisture and \nsouth winds will keep temperatures near climatology during the \nday and well above climatology at night. It is uncertain whether\nthis storm system will linger long enough across the western \nUSA to bring enough moisture and warmth to foster shower and \nt-storm formation. The later it gets in the fall the tougher it \nis to get large amounts of warm sector precipitation this far \nwest. If the storm system progresses eastward rather quickly \nthen the warm and moist conveyor belt would be across eastern \nKansas and points east per climatology. It is extremely \ndifficult to get large amounts of warm sector precipitation in \nfar western Kansas in November, but a little easier for central \nKansas. Thus, central Kansas locations are favored over the far \nwest.",
